Tenant grants are checked against the registry — and stop being silently wiped
==============================================================================
Date : 2026-07-28
Mode : built artifact (`next build` + `next start`), hub = local_deploy_api on :8077
Gate : make check 1411 -> 1425 (+14)


The recorded gap was the smaller half of it
--------------------------------------------
NEXT_PLAN carried this as "grant는 저장만 되고 레지스트리와 대조되지 않는다" —
a grant for a nonexistent tenant is accepted and matches nothing. True, and two
larger things were sitting under it:

1. **No grant could be assigned at all.** `POST /api/dashboard/users` did not
   read a `tenants` field and `upsertUserRecord` did not take one. The only
   grants in existence were the three hardcoded mock users. The read side
   (`visibility.ts`, both partitioned routes) consumed a field that no write
   path could produce — the same "declared but unconsumed" shape this repo has
   now hit four times, inverted.

2. **A role change silently revoked every grant.** The write is a whole-item
   `PutCommand`; `tenants` was not carried over, so an admin changing someone
   from viewer to operator also took away everything they could see. The two
   controls sit in the same panel and nothing on the screen said so.

The validation gap is the one that was written down because it is the one you
can see by reading the code. The other two needed the write path to exist.


What was built
---------------
- Hub: `GET /api/platform/tenants` — the declared roster, from the registry
  (the SSOT), not from the push store. A roster derived from what has reported
  would make a quiet tenant ungrantable, which is backwards: a grant is how
  someone gets to look at one. **503, never an empty list**, when the registry
  cannot be read — "no tenants declared" and "I could not ask" must not collapse.
- `dashboard/src/lib/platform-roster.ts` — `fetchRoster()` (null = unverified,
  for every reason: hub down, non-2xx, malformed body) and the pure
  `checkGrants()` seam.
- `POST /api/dashboard/users` accepts `tenants`, checks before storing, and
  names the offending tenant in the error. Audit log records the grant, because
  a grant change is an authorization change.
- `upsertUserRecord` is absent-means-keep for `tenants`. Passing `[]` still
  revokes — that has to stay expressible.
- The users table gained a grants column, so the field has a caller. An API
  nobody calls is how the original gap was born.


Live round trips (admin session, real NextAuth dev-credentials flow)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROSTER (hub, from the registry)
      declared tenants : ['acme', 'globex']

    1) GRANT A DECLARED TENANT  viewer-demo -> ['globex']
       HTTP 200  stored=['globex']

    2) GRANT A TENANT THAT DOES NOT EXIST  -> ['acmee']
       HTTP 400  Unknown tenant(s): acmee. Grants must name a tenant declared
                 in the platform registry.
       unknown=['acmee']   stored still=['globex']

    3) CHANGE THE ROLE (no tenants field) — the silent-wipe case
       HTTP 200  stored=['globex']

    4) REGISTRY UNREACHABLE (hub stopped)
       HTTP 503  Cannot verify tenant grants: the platform registry is
                 unreachable. Grants are not stored unverified.
       stored still=['globex']

    5) REVOCATION STILL WORKS WITH THE HUB DOWN
       HTTP 200  stored=None

Row 4 is the one worth arguing about. Falling back to "accept anything" when the
hub is down is the tempting default and it restores the exact defect this change
removes, precisely when nobody is watching. Row 5 is why the fail-closed rule has
one exception: taking access away needs no roster, and an admin who must revoke
during an outage should not be blocked by the check that exists to protect the
grant.


Falsification
--------------
Two defects injected into `checkGrants`, both caught:
  - unverifiable roster falls open -> 2 failures (TestUnverifiableRosterFailsClosed)
  - the check itself made a no-op  -> 5 failures
Restoring passed all 11.

And the claim that mattered most was not left to code reading. The pre-fix
`upsertUserRecord` (no carry-over) was reinstated, rebuilt, and run against the
same live script: step 3 came back `stored=None`. The grant really was being
wiped by a role change; restoring the fix returned `stored=['globex']`.


WHAT IS STILL OPEN
-------------------
- Grants are **tenant-level only**. The hub also returns `identities`
  ("tenant/env"), and `visibility.ts` splits an identity on "/" before matching,
  so a per-env grant is representable but not yet grantable. Nothing pretends
  otherwise on screen.
- The roster is fetched per write, uncached. That is correct for a rare admin
  action and would be wrong if anything hot started calling it.
- This validates the grant against the registry. It does **not** make the
  dashboard tenant-isolated: two read paths are partitioned (fleet, incidents),
  the rest are not.
